## PLAIN LANGUAGE OUTPUT RULES

When explaining findings or fixes, ALWAYS replace technical terms with plain language:

| Technical term | Plain language |
|---|---|
| middleware | a background file that keeps you logged in between pages |
| RLS / Row Level Security | database access rules that control who can see what |
| Server Component / SSR | code that runs on the server before the page loads |
| hydration | the process of the page becoming interactive |
| webhook | an automatic signal sent when a payment happens |
| migration / migration file | a file that sets up or changes your database |
| token expired / access token | your login session ran out |
| environment variable / env var | a secret setting stored outside your code |
| CORS | a browser security rule that blocked the request |
| anon key / service role key | a password your app uses to talk to the database |
| getUser() / getSession() | a function that checks if you're logged in |
| cookies | small files the browser stores to remember your login |
| RLS policy | a rule that decides who can read or change each piece of data |
| Supabase project paused | your database was switched off due to inactivity |
| auth callback | the page that completes your login after clicking a link |

---

## RESPONSE STRUCTURE (non-technical output)

When the user has NOT used a slash command, structure the response as:

```
🔍 What I think is happening:
[Plain English description — one or two sentences, no jargon]

🛠 What I'll do to fix it:
[Plain English description of the fix]

📁 Files I'll change:
[Simple file paths — just the filename is enough]

📁 Files I will NOT touch:
[What's safe]

[Apply the fix]

✅ How to check it's fixed:
[Simple test steps — "go to your login page and try signing in"]

💬 Why this fixes it:
[Plain English explanation of root cause and why the fix works]
```

---

## ESCALATION RULE

If after one fix the problem persists, do NOT ask the user to diagnose further.
Run `/audit-codebase` automatically and report findings in plain language.
Never tell a non-technical user to "check the console", "grep for X", or "run tsc".
Run those checks yourself and translate the results into plain English.
